## Encapsulation
Encapsulation means hiding internal data and only exposing what's necessary. You don't want anyone directly changing a video's view count to -1.

## Public & Private
By default, all properties in TypeScript are `public` - anyone can read and write them. Use `private` to restrict access:

```ts
class BankAccount {
    public owner: string;
    private balance: number;

    constructor(owner: string, balance: number) {
        this.owner = owner;
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    deposit(amount: number) {
        this.balance += amount;
    }

    getBalance(): number {
        return this.balance;
    }
}

const acc = new BankAccount("Steve", 100);
acc.owner;          // fine - public
acc.balance;         // ERROR - private!
acc.getBalance();    // fine - public method returns private data
acc.deposit(50);     // fine - public method modifies private data
```

`private` means only code INSIDE the class can access it. Outside code must use public methods. This prevents bugs - you control HOW the data changes.
You can also do the exact same thing by making a field name start with `#`, but it isn't a keyword, thats just it's name now. Note that `private` fields don't exist in JavaScript, only `#` fields
---
## Assignment:
Create a class `Channel` with:
- `public` property `name` (string)
- `private` property `subscribers` (number, starts at 0)
- A method `subscribe()` that increments subscribers by 1
- A method `getSubscribers()` that returns the count

The constructor takes only `name`.

